module fixture.closure.rustc_phantom_marker_non_applied_probe

// THE RED ARM OF THE POSITION PAIR. It is the construction
// `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode` `accepted_source_emits_uncompilable_target` files as its FIRST
// instance -- a coproduct's UNIT VARIANT standing in a NON-APPLIED type position -- committed here
// as a runnable file, which is the thing that row records it never had (its own specimen was
// produced by a lane whose fixture was never committed to this repository).
//
// WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS, STATED ONCE AND MEASURED. gunbc ACCEPTS this source with zero blocking
// diagnostics. Its emitted module contains an `Alpha` TYPE and a `Marker::Alpha` VARIANT. The
// field annotation binds the `Alpha` TYPE -- it is not a dangling spelling -- and returning that
// field from a function declared to return `Marker` is what rustc refuses, as E0308 at the parent
// boundary: `expected Marker, found Alpha`. So the rejection is at the RETURN, not at the
// annotation.
//
// THAT IS STILL THE FILED CLASS, and the two facts are not in tension: the source compiler accepts
// a construction whose emission the target compiler cannot build. DESIGN section 7 makes that the
// seed's floor rather than the target's problem, and DESIGN section 5 makes it silent wrongness on
// the source-to-target path -- below the ladder, not a rung on it. The only wall that fires belongs
// to rustc.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS COMMENT SAID THE ANNOTATION NAMED A VARIANT WITH NO TYPE TO BIND, AND
// THAT NO MARKER WAS MINTED HERE. Both are contradicted by the measurement recorded above, which
// was taken from this very file: an `Alpha` type does exist in the emitted module. The wrong
// account put the rejection at the annotation instead of the return and credited the emitter with a
// decline it did not make. It is named rather than silently overwritten because a plausible wrong
// mechanism is the kind of thing a later reader adopts (gunbc DESIGN section 3's concern about a
// carrier citing the wrong mechanism, not merely a stale name).
//
// WHY IT IS NOT IN THE RULED POSITION, WHICH IS THE DISCRIMINATION. `Alpha` here stands in a record
// field, never as an argument of an applied type node, so it is outside the position
// `rust_type_arg_identity_spelling` decides. The green arm beside it uses the SAME variant of the
// SAME coproduct as an APPLIED argument and its emitted crate compiles. The single variable across
// the two arms is the POSITION the identity stands in.
//
// E0573 -- expected type, found variant -- was PREDICTED for this fixture from the witness's
// ceiling row and NOT observed, and the reason is a property of THIS FILE rather than of the
// emitter: the markers are declared LOCALLY, so nothing here exercises the imported-marker
// construction where that row's two classifiers diverge. This pair is coverage of the POSITION
// question and does not discharge that row. The adjudicated expectation lives in
// `v1_compiler.emitted_closure_compile_host` `run_phantom_marker_identity_discrimination`, which
// also carries the full diagnostic.
//
// WHEN THE WALL CLIMBS -- type-position resolution consulting the TYPE namespace alone and
// refusing a name that resolves to a constructor -- THIS ARM FLIPS TO `SourceRefused` AND MUST BE
// KEPT (DESIGN 4b(4)). What changes then is this pair's expectation, not the fixture's existence.
// The names carry no domain semantics, for the reason stated in full at the control arm beside
// this file: shape only, and no leaf spelling shared with a real `std.measure` declaration, so the
// only variable across the pair stays the position.
type Marker = Alpha | Beta

type NonApplied {
  value: Alpha
}

fn non_applied_field_as_marker(subject: NonApplied) -> Marker {
  subject.value
}
